$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396781 1072 #uuid "8afd30f6-9418-5f9f-a8dd-bdb05d34a03e" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396781 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396782 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396783 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396782 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396784 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396782 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396782 1077 "field-1" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396782 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396784 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396782 1080 #uuid "35a58589-56cd-5637-b523-03f859fac5b6" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396783 1081 "even" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396783 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396783 1084 #uuid "d6fb8e02-c2ed-54c2-a769-c0f5e618e887" 13194139533469 175