db14dea0-146d-5df5-a79a-6c50bf00be55" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396811 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+4\\text{i})+(s-4\\text{i})}{(s-4\\text{i})(s+4\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396812 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396813 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396812 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396814 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] 